content.paragraph:
If $A = \begin{bmatrix}
      \textbf{a}_{1} & \cdots & \textbf{a}_{n}
      \end{bmatrix}$, with the columns in $\mathbf{\mathbb{R}}^{m}$, then $\text{Col }A$ is the same as $\text{Span}\{\textbf{a}_{1},\ldots,\textbf{a}_{n}\}$. Example 4 shows that the column space of an $\mathbf{m} \times \mathbf{n}$ matrix is a subspace of $\mathbf{\mathbb{R}}^{m}$. Note that $\text{Col }A$ equals $\mathbf{\mathbb{R}}^{m}$ only when the columns of $A$ span $\mathbf{\mathbb{R}}^{m}$. Otherwise, $\text{Col }A$ is only part of $\mathbf{\mathbb{R}}^{m}$.﻿

example.paragraph:
Let $A = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      1 & {- 3} & {- 4} \\
      {- 4} & 6 & {- 2} \\
      {- 3} & 7 & 6
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\textbf{b} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      3 \\
      3 \\
      {- 4}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Determine whether $\textbf{b}$ is in the column space of $A$.﻿

The vector $\textbf{b}$ is a linear combination of the columns of $A$ if and only if $\textbf{b}$ can be written as $A\textbf{x}$ for some $\textbf{x}$, that is, if and only if the equation $A\textbf{x} = \textbf{b}$ has a solution. Row reducing the augmented matrix $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      A & \textbf{b}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$,﻿

